  "image_name": "23997024786c074c1a9cfac5cc38263b.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes how carbon and nutrients move through the ocean food web and how microbes control whether that material is recycled near the surface or stored at depth. Phytoplankton at the surface take up CO2 and, using sunlight, form organic matter. That production then follows two main routes: it is grazed by zooplankton, or it is released and transformed into dissolved and particulate organic matter, shown as DOM and POM with carbon, nitrogen, and phosphorus components.\n\nA central message is the importance of heterotrophic bacteria and the “microbial loop.” Bacteria consume dissolved and particulate material, recycle it back into inorganic nutrients, and make those nutrients available again for primary production. Viruses appear to divert biomass into dissolved matter through a “viral shunt,” reinforcing recycling rather than transfer to larger organisms.\n\nThe figure also contrasts short-term recycling with long-term sequestration. Some particulate material sinks through the “biological pump,” while microbially processed carbon can contribute to a “microbial carbon pump,” both leading to long-term storage in deeper waters. Overall, the diagram emphasizes that microbes are not just decomposers; they strongly regulate carbon export, nutrient regeneration, and ocean-atmosphere CO2 exchange.",
